2026-11-05T08:23:02.454-08:00 [writer] SENT topic="s0036_drafts" correlation_id="9a604333-406d-4f9b-bb74-d6d0810f01cd" plan=["Confirm the leak by analyzing request headers in network traffic.", "Assess impact on authentication tokens and potential data exposure.", "Implement header removal or secure proxy configuration to prevent leaks."] draft="The severity of the requests Proxy-Authorization header leak (CVE-2023-32681) is **medium**. This vulnerability (VULN-0036-HW9-05, s0036_rel id 20078) affects requests versions >= 2.3.0 but < 2.31.0. It occurs when the Proxy-Authorization header is leaked to the destination server during HTTPS redirects, potentially exposing authentication tokens. While this leak could enable unauthorized access to protected resources, it does not compromise the entire authentication mechanism or cause widespread data exposure across all traffic. The impact is limited to specific HTTPS redirect scenarios where the header is inadvertently transmitted. The fix is to upgrade to requests 2.31.0. This medium severity classification aligns with the advisory, as the vulnerability requires specific conditions to exploit but does not pose critical systemic risks like remote code execution or total data breaches. (149 words)\n\nSource record: VULN-0036-HW9-05 (s0036_rel id 20078): requests affected >= 2.3.0, < 2.31.0, severity medium. CVE-2023-32681: Proxy-Authorization header leaked to the destination server on HTTPS redirects. Fixed in 2.31.0."
